# goodnight -- spread 17 of "So You Want To Build A Ghostwheel?"
# Copyright 2026 Corwin Brust; CC-BY-SA-4.0
# The last spread names every atom in the book with the day it was seen: a
# Goodnight Moon structure (naming everything in the room), nothing copied.
# The inventory below was gathered by a script over this book's sources, not
# by a C4 query: C4QL is "Not yet" (c4/README.md line 110), and the page says so.
kind: spread
number: 17
title: Goodnight
part: Part Two: how a ghostwheel wakes up

== picture page ==
Goodnight, wheel. Goodnight, rings, and the friends in the rings.
Goodnight, letters, and seventeen doors. Goodnight, floors, and the locks on
the doors.

Goodnight, star door. Goodnight, Spoke. Goodnight, Pin.
Each one has its day.

== layout ==
# The bedtime room is full of the book's things, so the words wait below it,
# read slowly, one goodnight at a time.
picture: top
words: below
size: 2.8

== fuller page ==
Goodnight, every atom: each small fact here that could be true this month and
false the next, with Pin's rows about it [rows]. Here they all are, by the day
each was seen [list].

Seen 3 November: @@DAY1@@.

Seen 4 November: @@DAY2@@; and this page's rows, list, days, c4ql.

Two days, one evening: by the clock in the shop, six hours behind the rows'
UTC, every one of them was seen on the 3rd [days].

None was gathered by asking C4. Its query language, C4QL, is "Not yet"; its
first piece selects entries by name, with its guards "STUBBED BY NAME" [c4ql].
A script read the facts instead. Goodnight, list.

== diagram ==
kind: pair
caption: Two witnesses to the same sightings: the rows keep UTC; the clock in the shop was six hours behind.
left: The rows' clock (UTC)
right: The shop's clock (CST)
@@CLOCKROWS@@

== facts ==
fact: rows
said: "The witness row. One line per sighting, seven fields, in plain words: atom ... said ... seen: when it was seen true, in UTC ... by ... from ... sha: the hash of that record at that moment ... state"
seen: 2026-11-04T00:37:47Z
by: part-two (claude-opus-5-5), reading the c4 tree on Avalon
from: c4/q/2026-11-03-atom-dates/PLAN.md, lines 34-42 (section 1)
sha: sha256:c7173ccf4ca322b30683f6ffd129ac34bbb97ebd03cce0e597f9ca5514353451
state: seen
note: The book's rows use these fields, with "fact" for "atom" and an optional note.

fact: list
said: @@LISTSAID@@
seen: @@LISTSEEN@@
by: python3 (spread.read_spread), run in the book folder
from: src/book, and the == facts == section of each spread it lists, door to wormholes
sha: sha256:@@LISTSHA@@
state: seen
note: The sha is of those sixteen spread files joined in book order (goodnight itself left out, since it holds this row). If another writer edits a spread after this was counted, the sha no longer matches: that is the gauge doing its job, and the list wants re-reading.

fact: days
said: @@DAYSSAID@@
seen: @@LISTSEEN@@
by: python3 (spread.read_spread), run in the book folder
from: the seen: lines of the spreads named in src/book
sha: sha256:@@LISTSHA@@
state: seen

fact: days
said: "His log pasted at 08:14 CST (14:14Z)"
seen: 2026-11-04T00:37:47Z
by: part-two (claude-opus-5-5), reading the c4 tree on Avalon
from: c4/q/2026-11-03-atom-dates/PLAN.md, line 105
sha: sha256:c7173ccf4ca322b30683f6ffd129ac34bbb97ebd03cce0e597f9ca5514353451
state: seen
note: CST is six hours behind UTC. The rows for Part Two were seen around 00:20Z on 4 November, which was about 18:20 on 3 November in CST.

fact: c4ql
said: "C4QL — introspection, reflection; looks. Not yet. h/c4qa.sh v0 implements selection and says in its own header that its three constraint guards are STUBBED BY NAME. No grammar card exists."
seen: 2026-11-04T00:19:40Z
by: part-two (claude-opus-5-5), reading the c4 tree on Avalon
from: c4/README.md, lines 110-112
sha: sha256:37fa9d9f44f031c05233c7481c3fd266b2e5cee688fd7bfb15e01dac377a2549
state: seen

fact: c4ql
said: "c4qa -- repeatable C4 introspection analysis over q/C4/ (C4QA), selected by C4QS ... Constraint guards (run-level -> indexed-only; named -> string-only; dual register invocations -> both) are STUBBED BY NAME below, minted when a guard first arrives. Born 2026-08-08."
seen: 2026-11-04T00:37:47Z
by: part-two (claude-opus-5-5), reading the c4 tree on Avalon
from: c4/h/c4qa.sh, lines 2-9
sha: sha256:a8882a602587be51d416df8a02a20e6e692d4366107b2fb01d2853df19e76e93
state: seen
note: It selects over q/C4/ only; it is not a query over atoms like these.

== narration ==
[picture A]
narrator: Goodnight, wheel.
narrator: Goodnight, rings, and the friends in the rings.
spoke: Goodnight, letters, and all seventeen doors.
pin: Goodnight, floors, and the locks on the doors.
narrator: Goodnight, star door. We'll come back.
spoke: Goodnight, Pin.
pin: Goodnight, Spoke. And goodnight to you.
narrator: Each one has its day.
[fuller A]
narrator [rows]: Goodnight, every atom. Each little mark in this book opens one, with what was said, when it was seen, who saw it, where it came from, and its hash.
pin [list]: Here's my list for you. Every atom on every spread, and the day it was seen.
spoke [days]: There are two days on it, Pin.
pin [days]: Door and Letters on the third. Everything else on the fourth, by the clock the rows keep. On the clock in the shop it was still the evening of the third.
narrator: A day is only as good as the clock that wrote it. So every row gives the full time.
spoke: And any of them could be out of date by the time you read this. That's why each one says where to look again.
narrator [c4ql]: We didn't ask {C4|C four} for this list. Its query language, {C4QL|C four Q L}, is the part meant to look things up, and it isn't built yet. When it can look, this page will ask it.
pin: Goodnight, list.

[picture B]
narrator: Goodnight, wheel.
narrator: Goodnight, rings, and the friends in the rings.
narrator: Goodnight, letters, and seventeen doors.
narrator: Goodnight, floors, and the locks on the doors.
narrator: Goodnight, star door.
spoke: Goodnight, Pin.
pin: Goodnight, Spoke.
narrator: Each one has its day.
[fuller B]
narrator [rows]: Goodnight, every atom. Each mark opens the rows Pin wrote: what was said, when it was seen, who saw it, which file, and that file's hash.
narrator [list]: The list names the atoms on every spread, each with the day it was seen.
spoke [list]: Pin, where do I start?
pin [list]: At the end of the list, Spoke. Then work back.
narrator [days]: There are two days on the list. Door and Letters were read on the third. Every other spread was read on the fourth, by the UTC clock, while the clock in the shop still said the evening of the third. Both are true.
narrator [c4ql]: {C4QL|C four Q L}, the part of {C4|C four} meant to look things up, is not yet built. Its first piece can select entries by name, with its guards stubbed. When it can look, this page should ask it.
spoke: Goodnight, list.
pin: Goodnight.

